
You can quickly report sightings --- and importantly, lack of sightings --- of mice using our survey form linked below.

We are seeking observations of mice within paddocks (not houses or sheds) from the general public to supplement our scientific survey data. This information will potentially improve our understanding and forecasts of mouse populations. 

You will be asked to categorise the level of mouse activity you’ve observed, date and location of the observation, how you observed mice and whether mice were recently baited or not (if known). 

Participation is voluntary. We do not think there are any risks involved with taking part in this survey. You may not benefit directly from taking part, but you will be making an important contribution our understanding about mouse populations. Your answers are anonymous and treated as confidential – they will not be publicly released. We will store this data on our secure servers alongside our scientific monitoring data. This survey has been approved by CSIRO’s Social Science Human Research Ethics Committee in accordance with the [National Statement on Ethical Conduct in Human Research (2023)](https://www.csiro.au/en/about/policies/ethical-human-research). If you have questions you can contact the project leader [matt.rees@csiro.au](mailto:matt.rees@csiro.au) or Executive Manager of Social Responsibility and Ethics on +61 7 3833 5693 or [csshrec@csiro.au](mailto:csshrec@csiro.au).

If you consent to take part, [access the form to report mouse observations here](https://mouse-forecast.getodk.cloud/f/pSxhfvMR8ElSL70KM52oSPSdeAgbs7x?st=vXkh0bVCMFL8MDb8X0tFALrdFMfq!2CyV3XdJpYz8Y8FsVsyiTpS1pFUYErLYgBP). 


